[{:seon.task/name "numbers-are-formatted-by-the-printer"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-10-23: src resists growth so agents reuse and improve the core parts rather than making one-offs (docs/decisions.md). What exists, sites read 2026-11-12: four number formatters beside the printer. ```seon.cost/num-text``` (the cost verdict's short form: 1.23G, 4.56M, 12.3k, a whole number below ten thousand, a dash for nil; 2 uses in seon.cost). ```seon.render.ui/fmt1``` and ```seon.render.ui/fmt2``` (one and two decimals, a dash for what is no number) and ```seon.render.ui/group3``` (thousands separated), 14 mentions in seon.render.ui. seon.repl rounds a sum of milliseconds to three places inline where ```seon.lib/r2``` rounds to two. The printer has its own for a large number (seon.print.select) and for bytes (the emit namespace's bytes-text, which seon.render.ui already calls through its bytes-str). Plan: the printer owns the number forms the others need (decimals to a given count, thousands grouped, the short form), as public functions beside its bytes text, each with a contract and a docstring citing docs/render.md#how-a-value-is-printed; the four functions above are deleted and their callers call the printer's; seon.repl's inline rounding calls seon.lib's. No output changes: the proof pins each old form's text on a handful of numbers (0, 9999, 10000, 1234567, 2.5e9, nil, a non-number) before the change and asserts the same text after, and that the four old names resolve to nothing. Cost: none; a formatter is a call. It deletes four definitions and adds at most three."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.print-test/one-number-formatter-is-the-printers}}
 {:seon.task/name "printing-costs-few-contracted-calls"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-10-30 (jolt/agent-costs, 6adfd086bf: bin/test arms what root arms at boot, so cost proofs count as an agent's child does): printing through the armed printer is most of some functions' contracted calls, each validated by its contract in root and agents. The entry points that measurement named (seon.ctx/printed-run, seon.ctx.wire/request) are gone since the context became records; the cause stands and is stated by `seon.print-cost-test/armed-printing-adds-only-its-contracted-calls`: an armed `seon.print/print-value` of a flat map makes exactly nine contracted calls an entry (classify and scalar for the key and the value, child, entry and member-indent, leaf-op for the key and the value) and 86 more whatever its size, so a context or a request pays a contract check per element. Plan: measure which printer vars take the calls (seon.profile/touched over that print at n = 100), then make the per-element step a local function of one contracted entry (as seon.merge/content-attr builds its predicate once a merge read), never an uncontracted var. Proof: that test's per-entry calls fall from nine to about one, its estimate with it."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.print-cost-test/armed-printing-adds-only-its-contracted-calls}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-one-printer-is-for-context"
  :seon.task/plan "What is wrong: code prints values where it should keep them as data. The owner, 2026-11-04: \"Printed data is rarely the right choice and I want us to be very suspricious of any code that is printing values. The only value printer should be the one value printer and that's for assembling context for agents\". Sweep of src, 2026-11-04, on the step 6 branch, grep of pr-str, print-str, with-out-str and seon.print's entries: 71 lines of code. Context or page through the one printer, fine, 28: seon.ctx/eval-messages, message-messages, page-message; seon.repl's response text; seon.server's tool text; seon.render's print-hiccup and nested; seon.render.ui's lists; seon.render.proc's root context; seon.web.html's fallback; my.agent's sizer. A value printed into what is kept, where data could be, should go, 15: seon.repl/record-details's :printed and :line; seon.repl's :seon.error/given; seon.agent's test failures (:expected, :actual, :got, :message); my.task/fail!'s :counterexample; seon.turn's message bodies (two); seon.contract/plainly (two: it prints what it cannot keep) and two of seon.contract's error values; seon.render.proc's detail. Words for a reader, an error's message or a form to run, each to read again, 24: seon.repl's edit! messages (five), seon.contract's explain text (four), seon.cost's verdict lines (three), seon.server's (four), seon.agent's (two), seon.ctx's search hint, seon.task's refused entry, my.schema's, my.agent's, seon.main's, seon.repl's stderr. A wire or a key, not a value printed, 4: seon.edn/encode, the one encoder; a schema form's text in seon.ctx's search; my.root's form sent to an agent; root's warm-up. What should be true: what is kept is data, up to a maximum the config states (the core's spec, with :printed and :line going), and what prints a value does it through the one printer, for a reader, when read. Change none of this before the core: its spec holds the first group of changes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.print-test/the-one-printer-is-for-context}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-rows-cut-reads-the-rest-by-key"
  :seon.task/plan "Point's ruling 2026-11-11 (a cut's form names a map's member by its key when the key prints back, never by its position, which moves when a member joins). Built on jolt/cutkey 7d3cb1e08c (taken out of printwords): a member's path is its key (seon.print.select, keyword, string or integer). Still positional: a rows cut (seon.print.select/fill-rows) reads the rest of a map as (drop n (get-in h path)) (seon.print.emit/continuation), so in a sorted map of ids the member past the cut is read by its place. Correct: a map whose keys print back is cut as (apply dissoc (get-in h path) shown-keys) or (select-keys ...) of the rest, read-rest extended for it. Proof: the cutkey test (a member joining leaves the form that reads member 7 as it was) with the rows cut in play, red on jolt/cutkey as it stands (seen 2026-11-11: (form two) nil)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.print-test/a-cut-reads-a-member-by-its-key}}
]
